<P>Hm…</P><br><P>Yes. I was. These men had no further use for these things. They might as well go somewhere useful, as opposed to burying them with the bodies. </P><br><P>The other one, the archer, wore the same banded coat, and leggings as well, under his breeches…</P><br><P>The third…</P><br><P>There was no third…</P><br><P>I counted again. I am not known to be good with numbers, but I can tell the difference between two and three.</P><br><P>"Kheldan!"</P><br><P>Khel explained. Somewhere in the frenzy of the fight, around the same time I had passed out, Kheldan and Aethani had squared off, mace against staff. Both had been unwilling to attack the other; Khel because of his odd chivalry at hitting a woman, and Aeth because she was really not there to hurt anyone. She had, in fact, come to despise the bounty hunters over the months of their travels. They were base, cruel men. Men who killed for their living, without regard to their victims. Gold was all they cared for. Right or wrong were irrelevant.</P><br><P>History aside, they rushed to my aid as I fell, and dragged me into the house to heal me, Wilitin Windwalker must have recovered from his pounding and staggered off into the woods.</P><br><P>Apparently Aeth’s mace had not shaken his brain up quite enough. I was still wounded and he had a large head start in the darkness. Wilitin was long gone. Even if my leg were whole I would be hard pressed to track him at night. </P><br><P>This disturbed me greatly. Now he was out there, and he knew where to find Gnasher. Would he come back for Gnasher, in revenge? Would he interrogate Gnasher to find me again?</P><br><P>The world was suddenly an unsafe and uncertain place again. I could not be everywhere at once. Just as I had not been there when Layla needed me, I could not be here when Gnasher was attacked. </P><br><P>What kind of world was this? It was madness. Was there no one that could be safe in my life?</P><br><P>&#9;I had run out of tobacco. Fortunately, Gnasher still had some left. The candles were burning low as I rolled the night’s last smokestick and gazed around in the dimness at my fellows.</P><br><P>Kheldan and Aethani had laid their bedrolls down on the floor, while Gnasher was curled in his smelly blanket on the bed. Gnasher and Khel’s snoring filled the room and Aeth had the top of her bedroll pulled up over her head to try and muffle the noise.</P><br><P>I smiled and laughed softly to myself. Things had changed a lot for me in the last few years. I should be raising little ones to be great hunters, with my lovely wife, in our lovely house above the glade.</P><br><P>Instead, I was unshaven and unbathed for days, sitting at the table of a gnoll, with blood still caked in my clothes; my blood and others, mixed. I had a bounty on my head…</P><br><P>Not quite the heroism I had imagined in my youth.</P><br><P>But somehow it was not so bad.</P><br><P>The situation had a strange charm to it somehow.</P><br><P>Before me on the table, the soft yellow candlelight dancing over it, was the letter from Felwithe that Aethani had given me. The wax seal was unbroken on it, but its design was unfamiliar to me.</P><br><P>I turned the letter over in my hands and felt the grain of the parchment under my fingertips. Felwithe was an elf city far in the lands of Faydwer. </P>         <br>
